	abstract = {Despite excellent theoretical support, Diﬀerential Privacy (DP) can still be a challenge to implement in practice. In part, this challenge is due to the very real concerns associated with converting arbitrary or inﬁnite-precision theoretical mechanisms to the often messy realities of ﬂoating point or ﬁxed-precision. Beginning with the troubling result of Mironov demonstrating the security issues of using ﬂoating point for implementing the Laplace mechanism, there have been many reasonable concerns raised on the vulnerabilities of real-world implementations of DP.},
